 Great write‑up, Truong. This is one of the most complete interview guides I've seen.

 One angle I'd add to the "evaluation" section: governance and harness versioning.

 In my experience, the gap between "works in the notebook" and "can be trusted in production" is exactly the gap between a model and the system around it. I've been building a stack (ALICE) where the router checks a live map and memory before calling the LLM — if a procedure already exists, the model doesn't even get called. The LLM is one component, not the system.

 That changes how you think about evaluation too. You're not just evaluating the model. You're evaluating the decision path — when did the router choose memory vs. the LLM? What guardrails (permissions, security, budget) applied? Which harness version produced this result?

 The article rightly points out that evaluation is a differentiator. I'd sharpen that: evaluating the harness, not just the model, is the differentiator. It's what separates a prototype from a platform.

